  style.
    .masonry-grid {
      column-count: 2;
    }
    @media (max-width: 768px) {
      .masonry-grid {
        column-count: 1;
      }
    }
    .masonry-grid .card {
      break-inside: avoid;
      margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    }
    .card-body {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
    }

  .masonry-grid
    .card
      img.card-img-top(src="/images/astrophotography/starnighs-presentation.jpg" alt="Starnights presentation")
      .card-body
        h5.card-title DIY Barn door tracker
        p.lead What is it?
        p.card-text
          | A barn door tracker, also known as a "volgplank" in Dutch, is a simple device used in
          | astrophotography to counteract the Earth's rotation. It's designed to allow a camera or
          | telescope to capture long-exposure images of the night sky without stars appearing as
          | streaks due to Earth's movement.

    .card
      img.card-img-top(src="/images/astrophotography/project-boxes.jpg" alt="Project boxes")
      .card-body
        h5.card-title The code that makes the project "tick"
        p.card-text
          | The barn door tracker operates using a stepper motor, which advances a set distance with
          | each step. This means someone needs to send step commands at the correct interval. This
          | seemingly simple task took more work than expected. For instance, issuing step commands at a
          | constant interval doesn't result in a uniform rotation due to the barn door tracker's unique
          | geometry. When to step is calculated by tiny microcontrollers, such as the Arduino Nano and
          | Xiao Seed BLE. These microcontrollers run code written in CircuitPython and C++ (Arduino
          | variant), which you can access in the linked repository.
        a.btn(href="https://github.com/MateoVanDamme/barn-door-tracker" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="background-color: rgb(51, 51, 51); color: white; border: none; margin-top: auto; align-self: flex-start;")
          svg(xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="currentColor" class="bi bi-github" viewBox="0 0 16 16" style="margin-right: 6px;")
            path(d="M8 0C3.58 0 0 3.58 0 8c0 3.54 2.29 6.53 5.47 7.59.4.07.55-.17.55-.38 0-.19-.01-.82-.01-1.49-2.01.37-2.53-.49-2.69-.94-.09-.23-.48-.94-.82-1.13-.28-.15-.68-.52-.01-.53.63-.01 1.08.58 1.23.82.72 1.21 1.87.87 2.33.66.07-.52.28-.87.51-1.07-1.78-.2-3.64-.89-3.64-3.95 0-.87.31-1.59.82-2.15-.08-.2-.36-1.02.08-2.12 0 0 .67-.21 2.2.82.64-.18 1.32-.27 2-.27.68 0 1.36.09 2 .27 1.53-1.04 2.2-.82 2.2-.82.44 1.1.16 1.92.08 2.12.51.56.82 1.27.82 2.15 0 3.07-1.87 3.75-3.65 3.95.29.25.54.73.54 1.48 0 1.07-.01 1.93-.01 2.2 0 .21.15.46.55.38A8.012 8.012 0 0 0 16 8c0-4.42-3.58-8-8-8z")
          | Microcontroller Code

    .card
      img.card-img-top.mx-auto.d-block(src="/images/astrophotography/movement.gif" alt="Tracker Movement" style="width: 300px;")
      .card-body
        h5.card-title Kinematica
        p.card-text
          | The barn door tracker consists of two hinged boards, with one board acting as a fixed
          | base and the other as a movable lid or door. The camera or telescope is mounted on the
          | movable lid. A hinge connects the two boards at one end, and the other end of the
          | movable board has a threaded rod.
          br
          br
          | As the Earth rotates, a motor makes the threaded rod turn. This causes the movable lid
          | to open at a rate that matches the Earth's rotation. Because the camera/telescope is
          | mounted on the movable lid, it now also turns. This allows for
          | long-exposure photography without star trailing.

    .card.text-center
      .card-body
        h5.card-title Presentation slides
        p.card-text
          | This presentation explains the purpose of my tracker, and what makes it tick on the inside.
          | It was used for my talks at Starnights and VVS Scheldeland.
